{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS1pgubiGkKbFDej3FC4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016ZELsgbNsFcVvFnaGjzRao","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/h-z1BWBn; python3 - <<'E'\np='src/app.py'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"import time\\n\",\"import time\\nfrom decimal import Decimal\\n\",1)\ns=s.replace('''def publish(table''','''def to_dynamo(value):\n    \"\"\"Recursively turn floats into Decimal; boto3 rejects float attributes.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, float):\n        return Decimal(str(value))\n    if isinstance(value, dict):\n        return {k: to_dynamo(v) for k, v in value.items()}\n    if isinstance(value, (list, tuple)):\n        return [to_dynamo(v) for v in value]\n    return value\n\n\ndef publish(table''',1)\ns=s.replace(\"table.put_item(Item=item)\",\"table.put_item(Item=to_dynamo(item))\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='tests/test_publish.py'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('''\n\nif __name__''','''\n    def test_second_publish_stores_similarity_scores(self):\n        table = FakeTable()\n        base = {\"title\": \"T\", \"tags\": [\"cron\", \"linux\"], \"body\": \"two crontabs ran the line\"}\n        app.publish(table, dict(base, slug=\"first-story\"))\n        app.publish(table, dict(base, slug=\"second-story\", tags=[\"cron\"]))\n        similar = table.get_item(Key={\"slug\": \"second-story\"})[\"Item\"][\"similar\"]\n        self.assertEqual(similar[0][\"slug\"], \"first-story\")\n        self.assertGreater(similar[0][\"score\"], 0)\n\n\nif __name__''',1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Why the second publish returns 500\n\n**Cause.** `publish()` stores `similar: [{slug, score}]`, and `score` comes from `similarity()`, which returns a Python `float` (`round(..., 4)`). boto3's DynamoDB serializer rejects floats (`TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.`). The first publish succeeds only because the library is empty, so `similar` is `[]` and no float is ever written. From the second publish on, `similar` contains scores and `put_item` fails. `handler()` turns the exception into a 500.\n\n**Fix.** `src/app.py` now passes the item through `to_dynamo()`, which recursively converts floats to `Decimal(str(x))`, before `put_item`. Converting at the storage boundary means any float added to the item later is also handled. `tests/test_publish.py` has a new regression test that publishes two stories in a row. It fails without the fix, because the fake table validates items the way boto3 does.\n\n**Notes (not changed).** `publish()` reads existing stories with a single `scan`, which stops at 1 MB. Once the library is large, the related list will only consider the first page of results.\nE\npython3 -m unittest discover tests 2>&1 | tail -3"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2295,"cache_read_input_tokens":21298,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2295},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d94ed7a-e3d5-46e7-9f6d-de50f9911244","uuid":"b16aaaf5-610b-4f69-a9ea-56e6d100338b","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:15:40.359Z","request_id":"req_011CgS1pgfxGwVv2YhQ1Y5xx","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016ZELsgbNsFcVvFnaGjzRao":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/h-z1BWBn; python3 - <<'E'\np='src/app.py'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"import time\\n\",\"import time\\nfrom decimal import Decimal\\n\",1)\ns=s.replace('''def publish(table''','''def to_dynamo(value):\n    \"\"\"Recursively turn floats into Decimal; boto3 rejects float attributes.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, float):\n        return Decimal(str(value))\n    if isinstance(value, dict):\n        return {k: to_dynamo(v) for k, v in value.items()}\n    if isinstance(value, (list, tuple)):\n        return [to_dynamo(v) for v in value]\n    return value\n\n\ndef publish(table''',1)\ns=s.replace(\"table.put_item(Item=item)\",\"table.put_item(Item=to_dynamo(item))\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='tests/test_publish.py'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('''\n\nif __name__''','''\n    def test_second_publish_stores_similarity_scores(self):\n        table = FakeTable()\n        base = {\"title\": \"T\", \"tags\": [\"cron\", \"linux\"], \"body\": \"two crontabs ran the line\"}\n        app.publish(table, dict(base, slug=\"first-story\"))\n        app.publish(table, dict(base, slug=\"second-story\", tags=[\"cron\"]))\n        similar = table.get_item(Key={\"slug\": \"second-story\"})[\"Item\"][\"similar\"]\n        self.assertEqual(similar[0][\"slug\"], \"first-story\")\n        self.assertGreater(similar[0][\"score\"], 0)\n\n\nif __name__''',1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Why the second publish returns 500\n\n**Cause.** `publish()` stores `similar: [{slug, score}]`, and `score` comes from `similarity()`, which returns a Python `float` (`round(..., 4)`). boto3's DynamoDB serializer rejects floats (`TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.`). The first publish succeeds only because the library is empty, so `similar` is `[]` and no float is ever written. From the second publish on, `similar` contains scores and `put_item` fails. `handler()` turns the exception into a 500.\n\n**Fix.** `src/app.py` now passes the item through `to_dynamo()`, which recursively converts floats to `Decimal(str(x))`, before `put_item`. Converting at the storage boundary means any float added to the item later is also handled. `tests/test_publish.py` has a new regression test that publishes two stories in a row. It fails without the fix, because the fake table validates items the way boto3 does.\n\n**Notes (not changed).** `publish()` reads existing stories with a single `scan`, which stops at 1 MB. Once the library is large, the related list will only consider the first page of results.\nE\npython3 -m unittest discover tests 2>&1 | tail -3"}}}
